---
description: >
  Error recovery for the local UnrealMCP tools. Applies when a tool call returns
  an error, an unexpected result, or a partial failure.
alwaysApply: false
globs: "**/*"
---

"cannot reach Unreal": the editor isn't running, the UnrealMCP plugin isn't enabled, or the port differs (`UNREAL_MCP_PORT`). Ask the user to check.

"Unknown command: X": the editor has an older plugin build. Run `unreal_status` to see which commands it supports, then use `execute_python` as a fallback or ask the user to rebuild the plugin.

"unauthorized": `UNREAL_MCP_TOKEN` differs between the MCP server and the editor environment.

"timed out waiting for the game thread": a modal dialog is probably open in the editor, or a long compile is running. Ask the user to dismiss it. Don't blindly retry mutating commands.

`execute_python` returned `success: false` with a `traceback`: read the last line. For AttributeError or TypeError, look up the right name with `unreal_api_search` / `unreal_api_doc` and fix only that line.

"has no property 'X'": run `object_describe(path, filter="X")` to see the real property names. Component properties go through the component, e.g. `LightComponent.Intensity`.

Blueprint compile errors: read `editor_log(since_seq=..., min_verbosity="Warning")`, fix the specific node or connection, and recompile. Don't rebuild the whole graph.

"pin 'execute' not found" on a pure node: pure nodes (getters, math, `K2_GetActorLocation`, make/break struct) have no exec pins. Wire data only.

If a change made things worse: `editor_action(action="undo")`.

After 3 failed attempts on the same operation, stop and ask the user.
